WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.162
True position, part four. Material modifiers, and bonus tolerance.

2
00:00:08.866 --> 00:00:10.950
This hole can be anywhere from five hundred

3
00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:12.982
to five oh five thousandths in diameter.

4
00:00:13.066 --> 00:00:19.000
At its smallest, the most metal is left on the part. That's maximum material condition,

5
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:22.399
or M M C. For a hole, M M C is the smallest size.

6
00:00:22.483 --> 00:00:26.711
At its largest, the least metal is left. That's least material

7
00:00:26.811 --> 00:00:30.480
condition, L M C. For a hole, that's the biggest size.

8
00:00:30.566 --> 00:00:33.247
For a pin or a shaft, it's the other way around: M

9
00:00:33.347 --> 00:00:36.528
M C is the largest pin, because that leaves the most metal.

10
00:00:37.716 --> 00:00:42.192
With no modifier in the frame, the tolerance applies regardless of feature

11
00:00:42.292 --> 00:00:46.025
size, called R F S. Under rule number two, that's the default.

12
00:00:46.116 --> 00:00:48.778
Make the hole bigger or smaller, and the

13
00:00:48.878 --> 00:00:52.161
zone doesn't change. It's always ten thousandths.

14
00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:55.067
On a chart of allowed position against hole

15
00:00:55.167 --> 00:00:56.900
size, R F S is a flat line.

16
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:01.604
Now add the M M C modifier: a circled M after the tolerance.

17
00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:05.248
At M M C, five hundred, the zone is ten thousandths. As

18
00:01:05.348 --> 00:01:08.499
the hole grows away from M M C, the zone grows by

19
00:01:08.599 --> 00:01:12.878
the same amount. At five oh five, the zone is fifteen thousandths.

20
00:01:12.966 --> 00:01:16.765
The extra is called bonus tolerance. Bonus equals the

21
00:01:16.865 --> 00:01:19.561
actual hole size minus the M M C size.

22
00:01:19.661 --> 00:01:23.680
Allowed position is the stated tolerance plus the bonus.

23
00:01:24.566 --> 00:01:28.023
A real part. The hole measures five oh three five.

24
00:01:28.123 --> 00:01:32.861
Delta X is five thousandths, delta Y is thirty-five ten-thousandths.

25
00:01:32.961 --> 00:01:36.489
Position comes out to twelve point two thousandths.

26
00:01:36.583 --> 00:01:39.680
Under R F S, that fails: twelve point two is more

27
00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:42.748
than ten. But at M M C, the hole is thirty-five

28
00:01:42.848 --> 00:01:46.924
ten-thousandths bigger than M M C. That's the bonus. The allowed

29
00:01:47.024 --> 00:01:50.839
zone is thirteen point five thousandths, so the part passes.

30
00:01:50.933 --> 00:01:53.888
Always measure the actual size of the hole first.

31
00:01:53.988 --> 00:01:56.444
Without it, you can't work out the bonus.

32
00:01:57.633 --> 00:02:01.470
Why is that bonus safe? Take the smallest hole, five hundred, and

33
00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:06.317
the largest position error allowed at that size, ten thousandths. The worst-case

34
00:02:06.417 --> 00:02:09.891
space left inside is a perfect pin four ninety in diameter.

35
00:02:09.983 --> 00:02:13.555
That boundary is called the virtual condition. It's the

36
00:02:13.655 --> 00:02:16.225
M M C size minus the position tolerance.

37
00:02:16.316 --> 00:02:19.139
Now make the hole bigger. A five oh five hole can

38
00:02:19.239 --> 00:02:22.600
move seven and a half thousandths off center, and it still

39
00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:26.299
clears the same pin. That extra movement is exactly the bonus.

40
00:02:26.383 --> 00:02:28.268
This is the M M C idea. As long as

41
00:02:28.368 --> 00:02:31.771
the hole's surface never crosses the four ninety boundary at

42
00:02:31.871 --> 00:02:34.982
true position, a fastener of that size will always fit.

43
00:02:36.166 --> 00:02:40.174
That's why a functional gauge works. It has the datum surfaces, and

44
00:02:40.274 --> 00:02:43.976
a pin at the virtual condition size, sitting at true position.

45
00:02:44.066 --> 00:02:46.663
If the part drops onto the gauge, seated on the

46
00:02:46.763 --> 00:02:50.508
datums, position is good, with all the bonus counted automatically.

47
00:02:50.608 --> 00:02:52.689
Gauges like this only work with M M C.

48
00:02:53.883 --> 00:02:57.084
The opposite modifier is L M C, a circled L. It's used

49
00:02:57.184 --> 00:03:00.508
to protect a minimum wall, like a hole close to an edge.

50
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:04.414
Now the bonus goes the other way. The zone is ten thousandths at

51
00:03:04.514 --> 00:03:08.084
L M C, the largest hole, and grows as the hole gets smaller.

52
00:03:08.183 --> 00:03:11.694
Watch the minimum wall while the hole changes size, with the

53
00:03:11.794 --> 00:03:14.943
hole pushed to the edge of its zone. It never changes.

54
00:03:15.043 --> 00:03:17.411
L M C guarantees the wall, at every size.

55
00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:22.070
Some prints use zero position tolerance at M M C. Here's our

56
00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:25.223
hole the normal way, and the same hole with zero at M

57
00:03:25.323 --> 00:03:28.139
M C, and the size range opened up to four ninety.

58
00:03:28.233 --> 00:03:32.518
Both have the same virtual condition, four ninety, so the same gauge and the

59
00:03:32.618 --> 00:03:36.556
same fit. On the chart, it's the same line, just extended down to four

60
00:03:36.656 --> 00:03:40.422
ninety. Zero at M M C accepts a small hole that's dead on location,

61
00:03:40.522 --> 00:03:44.057
and a big hole that's further off. It gives the shop more room.

62
00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:49.331
A circled M can also follow a datum letter. Here, four holes

63
00:03:49.431 --> 00:03:52.815
are located from datum B, a center bore, at M M B.

64
00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:56.433
The gauge pin for B is made at the bore's maximum material boundary.

65
00:03:56.533 --> 00:03:59.746
When the actual bore is bigger, the part can shift on the pin.

66
00:03:59.833 --> 00:04:03.746
That's called datum shift. The whole pattern moves together, so it

67
00:04:03.846 --> 00:04:06.847
isn't extra tolerance on each hole by itself. C M M

68
00:04:06.947 --> 00:04:10.860
software has to fit the pattern within the shift that's available.

69
00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:13.532
The two thousand nine standard calls it M M

70
00:04:13.632 --> 00:04:17.711
B, maximum material boundary. On older nineteen ninety-four prints,

71
00:04:17.811 --> 00:04:19.769
the same symbol was called M M C.

72
00:04:20.466 --> 00:04:22.727
Next, in part five: threaded holes, and

73
00:04:22.827 --> 00:04:25.209
the complications they bring to position.
